s your Standing, and each Node is connected to a Structural or Hierarchical Node; active and connective nodes are only relevant to what Nodes a Privilege applies its benefits to.<br /> <br /> By default, the Mapping the Conspiracy system means every Node connects to at least one other Node and Privileges apply to the first Temporal and/or Exploitative Nodes that exist outward along their set of connected Nodes; you follow the links between connective Nodes (branching where multiple direct connections exist) until you hit an active Node, and the Privilege doesn't apply to any active Nodes that are only accessible by going through that one. In your example, all six active Nodes have an unbroken line of connective Nodes linking them to all nine connective Nodes, and each Privilege would have a higher/broader bonus since none of the Nodes providing the Privileges is directly connected to five or more other Nodes.<br /> <br /> Worth noting that, while it's perfectly okay to use this particular structure (it's not substantially different from the Hierarchical Pyramids example), it <i>does</i> mean that there's only two degrees of separation between the outer ring and each Hierarchical Node, and there being multiple Hierarchical Nodes means this conspiracy with as many Nodes as is minimally required for Standing 7 is susceptible to taking aggravated damage from taking out their leadership (to say nothing of the possibility of subversion).<br /> <br /> Whether or not having two extra Privileges is worth the risk is an open question, of course, but each Privilege only applies to one type of conspiracy action and not every Node is likely to be suitable for every type of action, so the scale of utility would largely come down to what the conspiracy has to work with.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","